<div class="mw-parser-output"><h1>Thief Role Play</h1>
<p>Heaven help the thief in trouble, few others will. Thieves are sneaky, sly, and have little honor. They have guilds and territories throughout the world, but most start their thievery in the small village of Udgaard. The mayor there is known to be friendly to them and even has one as a body guard. Thieves are everywhere except perhaps in the Healer and Paladin Guilds and some suspect that there may even be a few there. Below are some suggestions on how to play a thief. Feel free to send in any others with the <a cmd='#'>idea</a> command. Who knows, you may see it here!
</p>
<ul><li>Thieves work for various people from governments, religious orders, individuals and rebel groups. But one thing is certain, a thief is almost always working for his or herself first.</li>
<li>It is rumored there are thieves guilds all over Valhalla, that there is a town run by them north of Midgaard. It is true the old Guild in Midgaard was closed by the mayor sometime after Midgaard itself was rebuilt. It is rumored that Brother Kane had something to do with it and as such the thieves and the paladins do not get along well.</li>
<li>Some thieves are also assassins, but for the most part a thief will run rather than fight and as such they object to assassins leaving messy corpses in their way.</li>
<li>Thieves sometimes employ sorcerers to assist them. They rarely will employ a conjurer as their ability to conjure is sometimes too loud for the thief.</li>
<li>Thieves sometimes are employed as spies and information gatherers. Their skills in hiding and sneaking are perfect for such work as well as their ability to take and plant items without being seen.</li>
<li>Most thieves are very quiet about what they do. They may claim to be another profession and most do have some fighter or magical skills. It would be a poor thief that went around announcing to the world that they steal for a living.</li>
<li>Thieves are frequently hired for quests and adventures. They are useful in climbing walls and opening gates or doors for others. Many times they can find an entrance that others cannot no matter how hard they search. Many an adventurer owes his life to a thief who could pick a lock of the cell they were in. Even the stiff-necked Knights and Paladins have admitted in a tight spot a thief is not a bad thing to have around.</li>
<li>Most thieves prefer the city where there is lots of prey, fences, and places to hide. Some range the countryside alone or in small bands. Some are highwaymen, or marauders. A few go to sea and become pirates.</li></ul>
